  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nQuiet and dry weather conditions will persist through at least \nTuesday. Low-amplitude mid-level ridging moving over the area \nMonday will transition to broader WNW flow aloft by Tuesday. \nMeanwhile, a surface ridge will cross the area late tonight \nthrough Monday morning. With dew points in the low to locally \nmid 40s this afternoon, the combination of decreasing winds and \nclear skies tonight supports at least a potential for shallow \nfog across interior northern Illinois late tonight into Monday \nmorning. As has been observed the past couple days, this may \nultimately translate into heavy dew/frost instead of more \npronounced fog. Daytime highs will continue to run seasonably \nwarm (generally upper 50s to low 60s) through Tuesday.\n\nA substantial change in the overall weather pattern will lead \nto a noticeably colder and more active stretch of weather \nmidweek through next weekend. An increasing number of ensemble \nmembers are latching onto a compact wave crossing near or over \nthe forecast area on Wednesday. With the associated energy \nprogged to detach from a parent wave currently well west of \nBritish Columbia, the exact evolution of the system remains \nquite uncertain. However, the combination of a contracting low-\nlevel baroclinic zone below seasonably strong mid-level rates \nand within PWATs near or above the 90th percentile for mid-\nNovember supports a corridor of modest rainfall and perhaps \nembedded thunderstorms. While the severe weather potential looks\nto be quite low, cannot rule out small hail with any elevated \ndiscrete cells.\n\nWednesday's system will congeal into a developing long-wave \ntrough over the Great Lakes region late in the week as \nunseasonably strong ridging takes hold from the Rockies toward \nHudson Bay. This will create a favorable lake effect precip for \nnortheast Illinois into northwest Indiana Thursday and \nespecially Friday into Saturday. Precip type favors rain as ice \ngeneration will be an issue with minimum convective cloud tops \nof only -11C. Additionally, an extensive fetch over the \nunseasonably warm waters of Lake Michigan suggests near-surface \nthermal profiles will remain warm enough for primarily rain. \nWith all that said, it seems likely that some snowflakes will \nmix in with lake effect showers at times.\n\nKluber",
